# CODEOWNERS — the enforcement boundary.
#
# WHAT THIS CAN AND CANNOT DO, stated plainly because the CI boundary section
# in docs/ci/README.md depends on it and must not overstate it.
#
# There is currently ONE maintainer. GitHub does not count a PR author's own
# approval, so "Require review from Code Owners" cannot mean "a second person
# reviewed this" here — it can only mean the change is FLAGGED as touching the
# enforcement layer, and merged deliberately (by admin bypass, which is
# recorded). That is a weaker property than review, and it is the honest one.
#
# When a second maintainer exists, this becomes real review and the boundary
# section should be updated to say so.
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow. A `*` default rule would put every PR behind the
# same requirement without adding protection, and would make the bypass routine
# rather than exceptional — which is how a control stops being noticed.

# The CI enforcement layer. Every guarantee in docs/ci/README.md rests on these
# files; the guards cannot guard themselves, and that limit is documented
# rather than papered over.
/.github/**                                                @portava

# The Supabase target allowlist and the in-process chokepoint. A quiet edit to
# any of these removes protection with no check going red — check-guard-coverage
# cannot detect its own weakening.
/artifacts/api-server/src/lib/ciSupabaseGuard.mjs           @portava
/artifacts/api-server/src/lib/ciProdReadOnlyAuditGuard.mjs  @portava
/artifacts/api-server/src/lib/supabaseTargetPolicy.mjs      @portava
/artifacts/api-server/scripts/check-guard-coverage.mjs      @portava
/artifacts/api-server/scripts/check-memory-citations.mjs    @portava
